{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I understand the structure:\n- Reference data: 4183 cells × 11889 genes, with obs columns `guide` and `target_gene`. Guide IDs are in a format like \"SCAF4-I4\", \"LEO1-I2\", \"NUDT21-I3\" — guide names encode the target gene (e.g., LEO1-I2). So the reference data has labels for guide and target gene.\n- Query data: 3516 cells × 11889 genes, with only the `guide` column in obs. Guide IDs include \"NT-I5\", \"NT-I3\" (non-targeting control?), \"guide1\", etc.\n\nTask: Map the query perturbation groups (guides) to the labeled reference data. So, for each query guide, find which reference guide/target_gene its transcriptional effect matches. Then identify the query guide IDs corresponding to PABPC1, NUDT21, LEO1.\n\nMain challenge:\n1. Cell type shift between query and reference — the query's cell type composition differs from the reference. So we need to handle the cell type confound. Strategy: for each reference target gene, compute the perturbation effect profile (perturbed vs. control cells) in a cell-type-aware way, then compare the query guide's effect profile to the reference effect profiles. Or, first align/integrate query and reference in a shared latent space (e.g., scVI) and use a cell-type-aware mapping.\n2. Prevent leakage of target metadata: the query guide IDs may encode the target (like reference data's \"LEO1-I2\"), but here they're anonymized (\"guide1\", \"NT-I5\") — so we must not use the query's guide names to infer the target. Also, in the reference data, guide names encode the target — that's fine, that's the label. Leakage would occur if, e.g., we use the query guide's name to guess, or if we use the target gene's own expression. Hmm, \"prevent leakage of target metadata\" — probably means: when computing similarity between query guides and reference guides, we should not use information that reveals the mapping (e.g., the reference guide ID string itself, or the expression of the target gene itself). Actually more likely: the reference guide IDs literally contain the target gene names, so mapping query guides to reference guides gives us the label — that's the task. But we have to ensure the mapping is based on the transcriptional effect, not on metadata. Also, when computing the perturbation signature, we should exclude the target gene itself from the signature to prevent leakage (the most obvious feature that distinguishes, say, LEO1 perturbation is the expression of LEO1 — but that's not actually leakage, that's biology).\n\nLet me think about what \"target metadata leakage\" concretely means here. Probably: the query obs may have hidden metadata that reveals the target, but we shouldn't use any query-side gene annotation mapping. Or maybe: the reference var names match the target gene names, and we shouldn't use the guide-target correspondence from the reference guide names during training/mapping… but that IS the reference label. The reference is \"labeled\" — that's the whole point.\n\nI think the safe interpretation is: build the mapping using transcriptional profiles only, with the following controls:\n1. Use only reference guide/target labels as the label space.\n2. Don't use query guide names for anything except grouping.\n3. Exclude the candidate target gene itself from the similarity computation (so that similarity is driven by downstream effects rather than direct target gene expression) — this is a reasonable \"prevent leakage\" measure, and we can note it. Actually, a cleaner leakage-prevention step: for each query guide, when computing similarity to a reference guide targeting gene G, exclude gene G from the correlation computation. This prevents a match driven solely by the cis effect on the target gene's own expression.\n\nLet me check the data more: what are the unique guide IDs and target genes in the reference? What guides are in the query? Also check obsm (existing embeddings), X's type (counts or normalized), etc."}}
