{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"reasoning","text":"Now the overall picture is clear:\n\n**Reference**: 4183 cells, guides with names like `GENE-I#` (target gene names embedded in guide IDs — these are the labels), plus NT controls (NT-I1..NT-I10, totaling 922 cells). Targets are 33 genes + NT. Genes are RNA-binding proteins / cleavage-and-polyadenylation factors (this looks like the \"perturb-seq of RNA-binding proteins\" dataset — the task mentions PABPC1, NUDT21, LEO1).\n\n**Query**: 3516 cells, guides are `NT-I#` (controls share naming with reference NTs — interesting; query NT guides have the same names NT-I1..NT-I10) and `guide1..guide32` (anonymized perturbation guides, totaling 32 guides). Query NT cell count: 277+175+158+152+150+119+117+104+90+82 = 1424. Query perturbation cells: 2092.\n\nTask: map each query guide group to a reference guide/target via cell-type-shift-aware transcriptional phenotype matching. Identify query guide IDs corresponding to PABPC1, NUDT21, LEO1.\n\nDeliverables:\n- `output/guide_mapping.csv`, columns: target_gene, query_guide_id, score, runner_up_score, confidence\n- `output/analysis.py`\n- `output/report.md`\n\nMethod design (one primary method):\n1. Load both h5ads.\n2. Quality control: both are aligned (same gene count 11889, \"perturb.seq.align\" suggests pre-aligned to the same variable genes). Verify var index identity.\n3. Normalization: log1p(CP10k).\n4. Cell-type shift issue: query and reference have different cell-type compositions. No cell-type labels are provided! Only obs columns are guide/target_gene. So we cannot regress out cell type directly… we can infer pseudocells/clusters. Options:\n   - Simple and robust approach: compute perturbation effect as the difference between mean expression of guide cells and NT cells within the same dataset (dataset-specific controls exist in both). Then compare query guide effect vectors to reference guide effect vectors by correlation. Since both effects are differences against a same-dataset NT baseline, part of the dataset-level cell-type composition difference is cancelled out… but not completely: if the cell-type composition of perturbation cells differs from the NT composition within a dataset, the effect vector gets contaminated by cell-type differences. Also, if the cell-type shift changes the direction of the effect…\n   - Better approach: mutual nearest neighbor / scVI integration to build a shared embedding, then for each reference gene compute the perturbation-vs-NT shift in embedding space or in the corrected expression space; and for each query guide compute its shift in the same shared space; match by cosine similarity of shift vectors. Shift vectors in a shared latent space are more robust to cell-type composition.\n   - Even better, cell-type-aware: cluster the integrated data (shared cell-type clusters), and within each cluster compute per-cluster perturbation-vs-NT effect (if enough cells are present), aggregate, or use linear model with cluster covariate: for each gene, regress expression on perturbation indicator + cluster indicators → perturbation coefficient = cell-type-adjusted effect. Do this separately for reference and query, then correlate effect vectors across datasets. This directly handles cell-type shift as long as cell types are shared across datasets (shift = composition change, not new types).\n\nThe task says \"cell-type shift\" — i.e., composition shift — so clustering the integrated data and adjusting for clusters in the effect estimation is a reasonable primary method.\n\n5. Prevent target-metadata leakage:\n   - Query guide IDs are anonymized (guide1..guide32); never parse them as gene symbols.\n   - When computing similarity between a query guide's effect and the reference effect for gene G, exclude gene G itself from the correlation (cis effect of the target gene's own expression can dominate, and the target gene's expression is closest to \"target metadata\" — actually it's biological signal but excluding it makes the mapping more robust and prevents the match from being driven by a single gene). Hmm — is excluding the target gene the correct interpretation of \"prevent target-metadata leakage\"?\n\n   Another interpretation: the reference guide IDs contain target names (\"LEO1-I2\"), and query NT guides share IDs with reference NT guides (NT-I1..NT-I10 exist in both). If one naively matched by guide-ID string, one would get leakage / wrong mapping. \"Target-metadata leakage\" would be if the mapping procedure used guide-name string matching or query-side annotations that reveal the target. So the report should state: mapping is purely expression-based; query guide names are treated as opaque identifiers; NT guide-name overlap across datasets was used only to define controls, not to assign perturbation identity.\n\n   Also, when ranking reference targets for a query guide, the score must be computed from transcriptional effects only. As a leak-prevention measure, also exclude the target gene itself from the correlation (leave-one-out at the gene level), and note this.\n\n   Wait, one more potential leak: the reference has guide IDs like \"PABPC1-I1\" and query guides are guide1..guide32 — but could some query guide ID accidentally match the reference pattern? No, they're \"guide#\" and \"NT-I#\". The overlap of NT-I# IDs across datasets is real (same NT controls shared across replicates). Using NT cells as a shared baseline is legitimate.\n\n6. Quantifying ambiguity: score = similarity with the best reference target; runner_up_score = similarity with the second-best; confidence can be defined via the gap (score − runner_up), and possibly via bootstrap across cells. CSV columns: target_gene, query_guide_id, score, runner_up_score, confidence.\n\n   Wait — what are the rows of guide_mapping.csv? Presumably one row per query guide (32 rows for guide1..guide32, NT also possible?). target_gene = predicted reference target. Or the mapping might be reference-target → query-guide. Either way, per-query-guide rows with predicted target_gene is the natural output. NT guides map to NT. Should NT rows be included? The task is to \"map query perturbation groups onto the labeled reference… identify query guide IDs corresponding to PABPC1, NUDT21, LEO1\". Include all query guides (including NT groups, which map to NT) — or at least the 32 perturbation guides. Safer to include all query guides; NT will map to NT with high confidence, demonstrating calibration.\n\n   Hmm, but wait: are there more query guides (32) than reference targets (33 non-NT)? Reference has 33 perturbed genes. Query has 32 perturbation guides. Plausible: each query guide corresponds to one reference target, perhaps with one missing.\n\n7. Validation (lightweight, independent):\n   - Positive control: NT query guides should map to NT (i.e., NT-vs-NT effect vectors should look like noise and best-match NT).\n   - Cross-validation within the reference: leave-one-out guide-level — for each reference guide, treat it as a \"query\" (effect computed against ref NT) and check whether it maps to its own target with the pipeline. This validates that the method recovers known labels when there's no cell-type shift.\n   - Independent validation run: recompute effects with a different seed or slightly different similarity metric? The instructions say one primary method + one lightweight independent validation. Reference leave-one-out + NT control serve as validation. Can also run a second confirmation: correlation of query effects vs. reference effects computed on a different feature set (e.g., top variable genes) — that's part of method development, not a separate validation.\n\n8. Ambiguity quantification: for each query guide report score, runner_up_score, confidence = maybe normalized gap or softmax probability over targets from bootstrap. Let's define:\n   - score = Pearson correlation between query effect and best reference effect (computed excluding the target gene).\n   - runner_up_score = correlation with the second-best.\n   - confidence: gap-based, e.g., `confidence = score - runner_up_score` plus a qualitative label? The column is \"confidence\" — a single numeric. Can compute bootstrap over cells: resample query cells, recompute effect, recompute match probability. Expensive but feasible: per guide n_cells, bootstrap 50–100 iterations, recompute effect vector each time, take argmax each time; confidence = fraction of bootstraps where argmax == best target. That's a good ambiguity quantification and directly interpretable. 32 guides × 100 bootstraps × vector ops over 11889 genes — cheap. But bootstrap needs access to raw expression per cell — fine in memory (3516×11889 sparse matrix).\n\n   Actually simpler and still rigorous: confidence = bootstrap agreement rate. And report score gap in the report. CSV requires only the specified columns.\n\nCell-type adjustment details:\n- Integrate ref+query with scVI? Adds complexity/time, but scvi-tools skill is available and the environment presumably has scvi-tools installed. Alternatively, simpler: concatenate, normalize, select HVGs, PCA, then cluster with neighbors + leiden on combined data. Cell types should align across datasets (composition differs but types are shared — \"cell-type shift\" suggests same types, different frequencies). Then per dataset, fit a per-gene linear model: expression ~ perturbation + cluster covariates. For reference: perturbation indicator for each target gene vs. NT (one regression per gene per target). For query: perturbation indicator for each guide vs. NT.\n\nNumber of regressions: reference: 33 targets × 11889 genes ≈ 392k regressions; query: 32 × 11889 ≈ 380k. Each regression on ~4k cells with ~15 covariates. In numpy vectorized: for each dataset, we can do it in one matrix operation: design matrix D (n × (1 + n_clusters + 1 perturbation indicator))… but perturbation indicator differs per target/guide, so the design changes per group. Efficient approach: residualize expression by cell-type regression once (E_resid = E - projection onto cluster covariates), then perturbation effect = mean(E_resid[pert cells]) - mean(E_resid[NT cells])…\n\nCareful: residualizing against clusters (subtracting cluster means) and then taking pert-minus-NT means is equivalent to a regression with cluster covariates only if perturbation is balanced within clusters; strictly the coefficients differ, but the \"cluster-mean-centering\" approach is a standard, fast approximation (like computing effects within-cluster and weighting). More precisely: linear model y = Xβ, X = [intercept, cluster dummies, pert indicator]. The pert coefficient = weighted comparison adjusted for clusters. Fast exact way: for each group g (one perturbation group at a time), solve least squares with design [cluster dummies, pert indicator]. With ~4k cells and ~15 covariates, that's 392k small least-squares problems each of size 15 — actually each gene is separate y but design is the same per group: solve once via normal equations per group: β = (X'X)⁻¹X'Y, where Y is (n × 11889). Per group one matrix multiply: X'Y is (15 × 11889), solve 15×15 system. Very fast.\n\nSo: for each perturbation group (reference target or query guide), define pert indicator (cells of that group = 1, NT cells = 0, other perturbation cells excluded… or included as negatives?). Standard: compare group vs. NT only, excluding other perturbations. Design: intercept + cluster dummies (drop one) + pert indicator. The coefficient on the pert indicator is the cell-type-adjusted effect. This is clean, fast, and handles composition differences between the perturbation group's cells and the NT cells.\n\nBut wait: do clusters capture cell types here? This is a Perturb-seq of RNA-binding proteins in a cell line (probably HEK293T or K562). \"Cell-type shift\" — maybe the query is a different cell type or shifted composition. There might be cell-cycle effects too. Clusters on in