{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting point: the reference (ref) var includes target genes (like CPSF1), but the query (qry) var doesn't — instead, qry has other genes. This is an intentional leakage-prevention design: the query's feature matrix excludes the reference's target genes! Therefore, if we naively join on var, the target genes are missing from the query. This backs up the following interpretation: the mapping must not rely on the target gene's own expression (it doesn't even exist in the query).\n\nSo:\n- Shared genes: 11889 − 31 = 11858. The 31 genes that exist only in ref include 33 target genes minus NT... wait, 31 genes are ref-only. There are 33 reference targets (non-NT). Let me confirm: only_r's list starts with CPSF1..CTR9 — are all target genes ref-only? Check: do PABPC1, NUDT21, LEO1 exist in qry's var? If the query excludes all 33 target genes... but only_r has 31 genes. So 2 of the targets exist in qry too? Or perhaps some of the targets (e.g., SRSF3, THOC5) are included in qry. Let me verify precisely.\n\nThis design means: effect vectors must be computed over the shared genes (target genes absent in qry are excluded). For reference effect of target G where G is not present in qry, exclusion is automatic. For targets that do exist in qry, we should still exclude G from similarity to prevent cis-driven matching (leakage prevention).\n\nThis also clarifies \"prevent target metadata leakage\": the query's obs only has guide IDs, and the query's var excludes (most) target genes, so we can't cheat. Our method must not try to reintroduce target gene names from the query side; we use the shared genes.\n\nPlan adjustments:\n- Align on the intersection of var_names (11858 genes).\n- Compute cell-type-adjusted effects for ref targets and query guides separately within each dataset over the shared genes.\n- Similarity: Pearson correlation over the shared HVGs minus candidate target G.\n\nAlso leidenalg is