st target per query guide = argmax of correlation; score; runner_up = second-best; confidence: bootstrap agreement.\n   Bootstrap: for b=1..100: resample with replacement the cells of query guide (and NT cells too? Resampling NT adds baseline noise — resample both perturbation cells and NT cells with replacement within the same dataset), recompute effect via OLS, recompute argmax over all reference targets; confidence = fraction of bootstrap iterations matching the argmax.\n   For small groups (3–6 cells), the bootstrap will show low confidence — that's exactly the point (quantifying ambiguity).\n8. Validation (lightweight and independent):\n   a. NT negative control: pooled query NT effect (vs nothing? NT-vs-NT is undefined) — instead: treat each query NT guide individually as a \"pseudo-perturbation\" against the other query NT cells → compute its best-match score distribution; should be low scores near 0 compared to true guides. Similarly within reference: leave-one-out NT pseudo-perturbation.\n   b. Reference self-mapping: for each reference guide, compute its effect against NT (leave-one-out: exclude its own cells from... it's already vs NT only, so its own cells are within the perturbation side — no overlap issue; the reference consensus effect for its target is that single guide itself. Self-overlap trivially gives 1.0). Hmm — since each reference target has exactly one guide, the reference effect for target G is exactly that guide's effect; correlating a guide's effect with itself = 1. So within-reference validation must instead be: split-half — split the cells of each reference guide into two halves, compute effect from half A, correlate against consensus... only one guide per target, so cross-validation within reference: for each reference guide g with target G, compute effect from a random half of its cells; compare against effects from the other halves of the other guides; verify the ranking of G. That is: for each reference guide, half-split; query-side