C_rows (drop first column), p_ind]. β = solve(X'X, X'Y), Y = expression submatrix (rows × HVG). HVG=2000 genes → fast.\n\nBootstrap: 32 guides × 100 iterations × OLS each. Each OLS: n ~ up to 450 rows, 13 covariates, 2000 genes. About 640k flops per iteration × 3200 = trivial.\n\nReference split-half validation: 33 targets × ~15 gene-set correlations. Trivial.\n\nNow, one more design choice about the similarity feature set: HVGs selected on concatenated data (2000). Good.\n\nCell-type adjustment nuance: when both datasets are clustered together, do clusters capture batch rather than cell type? PCA on concatenated data with different compositions — clusters may be batch-driven. Still fine for composition adjustment within dataset? If clusters are purely batch indicators, within-dataset regression has no variation → dummy variables degenerate (per-dataset design becomes rank-deficient but lstsq handles it). Risk: over-adjustment that removes the perturbation signal? No — the perturbation indicator is separate.\n\nAlternative: cluster within each dataset separately. That way clusters = cell states within that dataset and directly adjust composition within the dataset. Actually, per-dataset clustering is simpler and avoids the cross-batch alignment issue for the adjustment step. But \"across cell-type shifts\" also implies the biology of the shift is shared; for mapping, we compare effect vectors across datasets — cell-type adjustment makes effects comparable. Let me cluster within each dataset separately (KMeans k=10 on each dataset's PCA). Hmm, but then the clusters have no correspondence across datasets — not needed for effect adjustment.\n\nThat said, it would also be nice to have integrated clusters to describe cell-type shifts (report figure/table: composition). Can do a joint PCA for the descriptive shift table, while using per-dataset clusters for adjustment. To keep a single primary method: per-dataset KMeans k=10 on each dataset's own HVG PCA for adjustment; j